**`.claude/settings.json`**

```json
{
  "hooks": {
    "PostToolUse": [
      {
        "matcher": "Edit|Write",
        "hooks": [{ "type": "command", "command": "your existing prettier command" }]
      },
      {
        "matcher": "Bash",
        "hooks": [
          { "type": "command", "command": "$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR/.claude/hooks/format-after-bash.sh" }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

**`.claude/hooks/format-after-bash.sh`** (then `chmod +x` it)

```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Format files that differ from HEAD (tracked edits and new untracked files)
# after any Bash command. Requires jq and git.
cd "$(jq -r '.cwd // "."')" || exit 0

files=$( { git diff --name-only HEAD; git ls-files --others --exclude-standard; } 2>/dev/null \
  | sort -u \
  | grep -E '\.(js|jsx|ts|tsx|json|md|css|scss|html|ya?ml)$' || true )

[ -z "$files" ] && exit 0

printf '%s\n' "$files" | xargs -d '\n' npx --no-install prettier --write --ignore-unknown --log-level warn >&2 || true
```

Notes:

- **It formats every changed file, not just the one the Bash command wrote.** Anything you have edited by hand and not yet committed will also get reformatted. If that's a problem, narrow the list. For `sed -i` you can parse the file operands out of `tool_input.command`, which is the JSON field `.tool_input.command`. Parsing shell commands is fragile, though, so the git-diff approach is the safer default.
- **`|| true` keeps a prettier failure from blocking Claude.** Exit code 2 would feed stderr back to the model; I chose not to do that for formatting.
- **Adjust the extension list** to match your prettier config and `.prettierignore`. `--ignore-unknown` skips unsupported types, and prettier still respects `.prettierignore`.
- **`jq` must be installed**, or swap that line for `python3 -c` to read `cwd` from stdin.

I haven't run this against your repo, so test it once: ask Claude to run `sed -i` on a file with bad formatting and check that the file gets reformatted.

A `Bash` command isn't visible to a matcher as a file write. The hook only sees `tool_input.command`, and `sed -i` or `cat >` can touch any file. So you need to find the changed files after the command runs. Use two hooks: a `PreToolUse` hook that drops a timestamp marker before each Bash call, and a `PostToolUse` hook that formats whatever changed since that marker.

**`.claude/settings.json`**

```json
{
  "hooks": {
    "PreToolUse": [
      {
        "matcher": "Bash",
        "hooks": [
          { "type": "command", "command": "\"$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR\"/.claude/hooks/bash-mark.sh" }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "PostToolUse": [
      {
        "matcher": "Edit|Write",
        "hooks": [
          { "type": "command", "command": "your existing prettier hook" }
        ]
      },
      {
        "matcher": "Bash",
        "hooks": [
          { "type": "command", "command": "\"$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR\"/.claude/hooks/bash-format.sh" }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

**`.claude/hooks/bash-mark.sh`**

```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Record when the Bash command starts so the PostToolUse hook can find files it changed.
touch "$(git rev-parse --git-dir 2>/dev/null || echo .)/claude-bash-start"
```

**`.claude/hooks/bash-format.sh`**

```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Format files modified (tracked or untracked) since the matching PreToolUse marker.
cd "$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR" || exit 0
marker="$(git rev-parse --git-dir 2>/dev/null)/claude-bash-start"
[ -f "$marker" ] || exit 0

changed=$(git ls-files -m -o --exclude-standard | while read -r f; do
  [ "$f" -nt "$marker" ] && printf '%s\n' "$f"
done)

[ -z "$changed" ] && exit 0
printf '%s\n' "$changed" | xargs -r npx --no-install prettier --write --ignore-unknown --log-level warn
exit 0
```

Make both scripts executable with `chmod +x .claude/hooks/*.sh`.

Things to know:

- **Only files changed by that command get formatted.** The `-nt` check skips files you were already editing by hand, so their in-progress state isn't reformatted under you.
- **Gitignored files aren't covered.** `git ls-files -o --exclude-standard` skips them, and so does the hook. Files outside the repo are also skipped.
- **`--ignore-unknown` skips non-prettier files** such as binaries, so a `sed` on those won't error.
- **Paths with spaces or newlines** are a known gap in the `while read` loop. Use `git ls-files -z` if that matters for your repo.
- **Config-based approaches don't work here.** The `if` filter can narrow a Bash hook to commands matching a pattern like `sed -i *`, but it can't tell you which file was written, so the mtime approach is still needed.

I haven't run this in your setup, and I didn't look at your existing hook config, so the `Edit|Write` entry above is a placeholder. Keep whatever command you already have there.

A `Bash` matcher can't tell which file a shell command wrote, so the reliable approach is to have the hook check git for files that changed. Add a second entry alongside your `Edit|Write` one in `.claude/settings.json`:

```json
{
  "hooks": {
    "PostToolUse": [
      {
        "matcher": "Edit|Write",
        "hooks": [
          { "type": "command", "command": "your existing prettier hook" }
        ]
      },
      {
        "matcher": "Bash",
        "hooks": [
          {
            "type": "command",
            "command": "cd \"$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR\" && git ls-files -m -o --exclude-standard -z | xargs -0 -r npx prettier --write --ignore-unknown --log-level warn"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

What this does:

- **Catches every write mechanism.** `sed -i`, `cat >`, `tee`, `python script.py`, and similar commands all show up as modified or untracked in git, so the hook doesn't need to parse the command.
- **Skips gitignored files.** `--exclude-standard` leaves out build output and other ignored paths.
- **Handles odd filenames.** `-z` with `xargs -0` copes with spaces and newlines in paths.
- **Ignores unsupported file types.** `--ignore-unknown` stops prettier from failing on files it has no parser for.

Tradeoffs to be aware of:

- **It formats every modified file, not just the ones Claude touched.** If you have unformatted edits of your own in the working tree, the hook will reformat them too. To limit it to files changed during the current command, add a marker: a `PreToolUse` hook on `Bash` runs `touch "$TMPDIR/claude-bash-marker"`, and the `PostToolUse` command filters the list with `find ... -newer "$TMPDIR/claude-bash-marker"`.
- **It runs after every Bash command.** `git ls-files` is fast, but if your repo is large, keep the command cheap or add an `if` filter so it only runs for commands that look like writes.
- **Files outside the repo are not covered.** Writes to `/tmp` or to other directories won't be formatted.
- **Formatting failures don't block anything.** A `PostToolUse` hook can't undo the command. If you want a failure to be visible, drop `--log-level warn` so prettier's errors are printed.

